<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Medina</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Medina is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the second holiest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> migrated there in 622 and established the first Muslim community. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> until Ali moved his seat to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>, and later rulers including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> claimed its protection.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/medina.jpg" alt="Medina" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Konevi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-Masjid_An-Nabawi_(Bird%27s_Eye_View).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Medina lies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hejaz">Hejaz</a> region of western Saudi Arabia. Its built-up area is surrounded by the Hejaz Mountains, empty valleys, agricultural land and old dormant volcanoes. Before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> it was called Yathrib, a name attested in an inscription of the Babylonian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> of the 6th century BCE and used in the Qur'an. According to Islamic tradition, Muhammad later renamed it Taybah or Tabah, and it came to be called Madinat an-Nabi, &quot;City of the Prophet&quot;, and al-Madinah al-Munawwarah, &quot;the Enlightened City&quot;, shortened to Madinah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The city has been inhabited for about 1,500 years before the Hijrah, or about the 9th century BCE. By the fourth century Arab tribes were encroaching from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, and around the time of Muhammad three prominent Jewish tribes lived there: the Banu Qaynuqa, the Banu Qurayza and the Banu Nadir.</p><p>Toward the end of the 5th century the Jewish rulers lost control of the city to the two Arab tribes, the 'Aws and Khazraj. In 622 Muhammad and an estimated 70 Meccan Muhajirun left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> over a few months for sanctuary in Yathrib. The longstanding enmity between the 'Aws and Khazraj was dampened as many of them and some local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Muhammad was agreed on as leader of the city. It served as the base of the early Muslim community, and a larger part of the Qur'an was revealed in Medina than in Mecca.</p><p>Later the city passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a>, whose governors dug the spring called 'Ayn az-Zarqa'a and built irrigation ducts and dams, leading to an agricultural boom. It was held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from 1254 to 1517 and then by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> conquered Mamluk Egypt.</p><p>In 1532 Suleiman built a fortress around it, and the Ottoman sultans repeatedly redesigned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Prophet's Mosque</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-of-mecca">Sharif of Mecca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/husayn-ibn-ali">Husayn ibn Ali</a>, attacked Medina on 6 June 1916, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the siege that followed brought food shortages, disease and mass emigration. The governor Fakhri Pasha held out until his own men arrested him and the Sharifate took the city on 10 January 1919. The Saudi forces conquered it on 5 December 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Medina is the second holiest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and the capital of Medina Province, with 1,477,023 inhabitants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophets-mosque">Al-Masjid al-Nabawi</a>, the Prophet's Mosque, was built by Muhammad in 622 and is the burial site of the prophet. The Quba Mosque is the oldest in Islam. The city also holds Masjid al-Qiblatayn, the Baqi' cemetery, Mount Uhud, site of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-uhud">Battle of Uhud</a>, and the King Fahd Glorious Qur'an Printing Complex.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Medina” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By repelling the Mongols and eliminating the Crusader strongholds, it stabilised the region and became guardian of Mecca, <b>Medina</b> and Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Abu al-Hasan copied the Qur'an in his own hand four times, sending copies to <b>Medina</b>, Mecca and the Al-Aqsa Mosque in Jerusalem; a fifth, begun at Bijaya for Hebron, was finished by his son after his dethronement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Selim I’s conquest of the Mamluk Sultanate in 1516–1517 brought Egypt, Syria and the holy cities of Mecca and <b>Medina</b> under Ottoman sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Prophet Muhammad died in <b>Medina</b> in June 632 CE without a universally acknowledged successor, and the uncertainty threatened to dissolve the young Muslim community before it had consolidated control over the Arabian Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>As custodian of Mecca and <b>Medina</b> the kingdom stands at the centre of Islam, and its oil at the centre of the world economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In February 1804 dan Fodio led his followers in a hijra, a migration explicitly modelled on the Prophet Muhammad's flight from Mecca to <b>Medina</b>, to Gudu, where his community acclaimed him Amir al-Mu'minin, Commander of the Faithful.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
